[MATH: \eqalign{
\sin(\theta) &= \dfrac {\text{opposite}} {\text{hypotenuse}}, \[5pt]
\cos(\theta) &= \dfrac {\text{adjacent}} {\text{hypotenuse}}, \[5pt]
\tan(\theta) &= \dfrac {\text{opposite}} {\text{adjacent}}. \
}]

Let's illustrate the given triangle.

[IMG: /graphics/d49e1afdb779b135f96a587896819f8e]

The tangent of an angle is equal to the opposite divided by the adjacent (T-O-A). Relative to [MATH: \angle B,] the opposite side is [MATH: \overline{AC},] and the adjacent side is [MATH: \overline{AB}.] Therefore,
[MATH: \eqalign{
\tan{B} &= \dfrac{AC}{AB} = \dfrac{\sqrt{5}}{2}.
}]
